Let me add one (and a half) more thought(s)...

Personally (I always let the RNG choose the characters randomly for
me) the ascended races (for example, similar for alignment) mislead
me a bit because the chosen races (or alignments) are not equally
distributed; if my math is correct we get (for example) on average

   60.273973% humans
   13.698630% gnomes
   10.958904% orcs
    8.219178% elves
    6.849315% dwarfs

Those numbers quite exactly match my personal statistics on the page;
but from the graphical representation alone (without knowing the above
numbers) I cannot derive much. The "problem" will become more relevant
if someone preselects some character criteria; one cannot easily see
the progress with individual races if the numbers are not normalized.

To get some impression of any personal difficulties with specific
roles, races, etc. it would be interesting to normalize the data of
the ascensions by the actually played number of roles, races, etc.

To summarize; I think two types of normalizations are interesting,
normalization by the random distribution factors (as presented in
the above table), and normalization of the individual ascension data
by the individually selected/played characters.

